{{Pie chart|thumb=right|caption=Religion in the Baliscan Confederation (2025)|label2=[[w:Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]]|value2=10.0|color2=Honeydew|label3=[[Catharism|Cathar]]|value3=9.8|color3=DarkOrchid|label1={{W|Irreligious}}|value1=50.2|color1=DodgerBlue|label4={{W|African diaspora religions|African Diasporic}}|value4=6.3|color4=Turquoise|label5=Other Christian churches (incl. [[w:Protestantism|Protestant]], [[w:Eastern Orthodox Church|Eastern]] and [[w:Oriental Orthodox Churches|Oriental Orthodox]])|value5=7.0|color5=Chartreuse|label6=[[wikipedia:Islam|Islam]]|value6=11.2|color6=DarkGReen|label7=[[w:Hinduism|Hinduism]]|value7=1.5|color7=Orange|label8=[[w:Sikhism|Sikhism]]|value8=1.4|color8=DarkKhaki|label9=[[w:Buddhism|Buddhism]]|value9=1.1|color9=Yellow|label10=[[w:Jewish people|Jewish]]|value10=1.0|color10=Hotpink|label11=Others/undeclared|value11=0.5|color11=Black}}'''Religion in [[Balisca]]''' is both diverse and deeply rooted in its unique history, though with an exceptionally high level of irreligion that distinguishes it from other nations in [[W:Latin America|Latin America]]. Polls indicate that an overwhelming majority of [[Baliscans]] are nonreligious, with over 50% identifying as such in the [[2025 Baliscan census|most recent census]].

While [[Christianity in Balisca|Christianity]] is the most widely professed religious family when counting all of its diverse denominations together, no single religion holds a majority within the Baliscan population. The largest religious groups are [[Islam in Balisca|Sunni Islam]] (11.2%), [[W:Catholicism|Catholicism]] (10.0%), and [[Catharism]] (9.8%), followed by a mix of other Christians (7.0%), [[W:African diaspora religions|African Diasporic]] (6.3%), [[W:Hinduism|Hindu]] (1.5%), [[W:Sikhism|Sikh]] (1.4%), [[W:Buddhism|Buddhist]] (1.1%), and [[W:Judaism|Jewish]] (1.0%) adherents. The Confederation has long adhered to a policy of [[W:Laicism|laicism]], with the [[Constitution of Balisca|Constitution]] mandating a "total separation of religion from public life" and protection of the [[Basic Law: Freedom of Religious Belief|freedom of belief]].

Balisca's unique religious landscape is a direct result of its history of "[[W:Convivencia|Convivencia]]". The country was founded by the early settlement of religious dissenters and persecuted minorities from the [[W:Iberian Peninsula|Iberian Peninsula]] and the [[W:Maghreb|Maghreb]]—including [[W:Morisco|Moriscos]], [[W:Sephardic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], [[Catharism|Cathars]], and [[W:Mozarabs|Mozarabs]]—who sought refuge from religious intolerance. Their shared experiences fostered an environment of pragmatic coexistence and cultural exchange, which continued for several centuries. This historical period was considered a golden age of mutual learning and tolerance, exemplified by the proto-Baliscan entities like the [[Hesperian League]] and its relatively liberal policies of religious tolerance. These diverse faiths and cultures not only coexisted but also [[W:Syncretism|syncretized]], giving rise to unique spiritual blends like the African diasporic religion [[Lucumí Tarica]].

However, Balisca's history of de facto convivencia was fraught with episodes of [[W:Religious violence|religious violence]], beginning with the [[Reapers' War]] and the [[Expulsion of the Tarracos|expulsion of Catalan-speaking Cathars]] from [[Estuaria]] by Spanish Catholics, and up to the [[Maroon Wars]] and the state policy of [[Maroon resettlement|Maroon removal]] in Bayara during the [[Baliscan Civil War]]. In the late 19th century, a growing [[Cosmocazo|oligarchic political system]] began to undermine the decentralized model of governance, leading to what historians have called a decline in this early spirit of religious and cultural harmony. The ensuing [[Baliscan Revolution]] (1918-1921), which was fueled in part by the increasing disenfranchisement of the diverse [[W:Proletariat|proletariat]] and opposition to this new elite, ushered in a wave of violent [[W:Anti-clericalism|anti-clericalism]] ([[Christianity in Balisca#Anti-clericalism and anti-Catholicism|especially anti-Catholicism]]) and [[W:Iconoclasm|iconoclasm]]. 

The Revolution did not merely seek to restore the old ''convivencia'', but to institutionalize it as a state-sponsored policy, defining Balisca as the first "[[W:Plurinationalism|cosmopolitan state]]" with a deep constitutional commitment to [[W:Laïcité|secularism]], [[W:Polyculturalism|polyculturalism]], and [[W:sectarianism|opposition to sectarianism]]. This ensured that the principles of tolerance and reconciliation became a cornerstone of the nation's legal and social framework, rather than being left to the whims of political circumstance.

